byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Planning appeal costs \u00a3130,000 with explicit warning of further expenditure risk","lead":"Appeal costs are inherently unpredictable and can escalate substantially. Multiple concurrent cases magnify the risk of blowouts.","iso":"2026-09-02","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Pay award negotiations unresolved with budget contingency now at risk","lead":"If pay negotiations settle higher than the 3.2% assumption, additional budget pressure will follow. Pay is typically the largest council budget item. Loss of contingency creates vulnerability to other unforeseen costs.","iso":"2026-09-02","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-02","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","headline":"Community Infrastructure Levy scheme abortive costs: Milford-on-Sea project redesignated, \u00a350,000 at risk","detail":"The Milford-on-Sea Seafront Regeneration Scheme has been redesignated from immediate delivery to a longer-term proposal for consideration by the future Mid-Hampshire Unitary Authority. This means development work already undertaken will be sunk cost and the council will likely need to fund \u00a350,000 of expenditure incurred to date from its own resources rather than recovering it from the scheme.","category":"capital","matters":"Scheme abandonment wastes development costs and capital funding. The redesignation signals the council's capital plans are being disrupted by local government reorganization uncertainty.","figures":"\u00a350,000 of expenditure incurred to date on Milford scheme","quote":"Cabinet agreed to redesignate the Milford-on-Sea Seafront Regeneration Scheme from an immediate delivery project to a longer-term proposal for consideration by the future Mid-Hampshire Unitary Authority. As a result, it is likely that NFDC will need to fund expenditure incurred to date of \u00a350,000 from its own resources.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-02","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","headline":"Planning appeal costs \u00a3130,000 with explicit warning of further expenditure risk","detail":"Three significant planning cases are currently under appeal. Costs to date are primarily legal advice, specialist consultant input, appeal preparation and attendance. The document explicitly flags that there is a risk further costs will be incurred.","category":"overspend","matters":"Appeal costs are inherently unpredictable and can escalate substantially. Multiple concurrent cases magnify the risk of blowouts.","figures":"\u00a3130,000 to date on three cases","quote":"Development Management (\u00a3130,000) - Planning appeal costs continue to be incurred in relation to three significant cases currently under appeal. Expenditure to date principally relates to legal advice, specialist consultant input and appeal preparation plus attendance and there is a risk further costs will be incurred.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-02","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","headline":"Pay award negotiations unresolved with budget contingency now at risk","detail":"The 2026/27 Local Government Services pay award remains unresolved. Union claim is for \u00a33,000 or 10% (whichever greater) with industrial action processes underway. Employer offer of 3.3% exceeds the council's 3.2% assumption by 0.1%, which the council says contingency can cover\u2014but only if the settlement does not worsen.","category":"governance","matters":"If pay negotiations settle higher than the 3.2% assumption, additional budget pressure will follow. Pay is typically the largest council budget item. Loss of contingency creates vulnerability to other unforeseen costs.","figures":"3.3% employer offer vs 3.2% council assumption, 0.1% gap. Union claim: \u00a33,000 or 10% whichever is greater.","quote":"There remains a financial risk that additional budget provision may be required, depending on the outcome of national negotiations and the final settlement and whether the remaining contingency budget can fund it.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-02","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","headline":"Investment property income \u00a380,000 lower than forecast due to vacancies","detail":"Commercial investment property income is underperforming forecast by \u00a370,000, with an additional \u00a310,000 in unexpected business rates charges for void periods. This signals asset portfolio underutilization.","category":"income","matters":"Commercial property income is often a core funding source for councils. A \u00a380,000 shortfall indicates the portfolio is not performing as expected and may signal broader property market challenges.","figures":"\u00a370,000 income shortfall plus \u00a310,000 additional business rates for void periods = \u00a380,000 total adverse variance","quote":"Investment Properties (\u00a380,000) \u2013 Income is currently anticipated to be \u00a370,000 less than estimate and additional business rates costs of \u00a310,000 are to be incurred for void periods.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-02","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","headline":"\u00a3161,000 supplementary budget required for mandatory electrical compliance works at residential park site","detail":"Stillwater Park is a 69-plot residential park home site owned and operated by the council. An electrical infrastructure assessment identified aging equipment requiring replacement and standardization of legacy connection methods to meet current standards. The council has statutory duties as site operator to maintain electrical infrastructure in a safe and compliant condition.","category":"capital","matters":"This is an unbudgeted mandatory compliance cost that cannot be deferred. Electrical safety failures could expose the council to legal and safety liability.","figures":"\u00a3161,000 total, 69 plots, approximately \u00a32,330 per plot","quote":"an estimated cost of c\u00a32,330 per plot has been established. Applying this across 69 plots gives a baseline estimated programme cost of \u00a3161,000","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-02"},"name":"New Forest","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Community Infrastructure Levy scheme abortive costs: Milford-on-Sea project redesignated, \u00a350,000 at risk","lead":"Scheme abandonment wastes development costs and capital funding. The redesignation signals the council's capital plans are being disrupted by local government reorganization uncertainty.","also":["4 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-07","n_findings":5,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report (based on performance April to June 2026 inclusive)","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.newforest.gov.uk/documents/s50154/50154.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":62,"news_drafts_total":3}}